## Resource-Oriented URL Design

```
RESOURCE DESIGN RULES:

1. Nouns for resources, not verbs:
   WRONG: GET  /api/getOrders
          POST /api/createOrder
          POST /api/deleteOrder?id=42
   RIGHT: GET  /api/orders
          POST /api/orders
          DELETE /api/orders/42

2. Hierarchical relationships:
   /api/orders                        All orders
   /api/orders/42                     Specific order
   /api/orders/42/items               Items in that order
   /api/orders/42/items/5             Specific item in that order

3. Keep hierarchy shallow (max 2-3 levels):
   /api/users/7/orders/42/items/5/reviews  ← Too deep
   Instead: /api/order-items/5/reviews    ← Flatten with compound resource

4. Actions that don't map to CRUD:
   POST /api/orders/42/cancel         ← Use sub-resource for actions
   POST /api/orders/42/refund
   POST /api/payments/42/retry
   (not POST /api/cancelOrder?id=42)
```

## HTTP Status Codes — Use Correctly

```
2xx SUCCESS:
  200 OK           — Successful GET, PATCH, DELETE with body
  201 Created      — Successful POST that created a resource
                     Include Location header: Location: /api/orders/42
  202 Accepted     — Async operation started (not yet complete)
  204 No Content   — Successful DELETE or action with no response body

4xx CLIENT ERRORS:
  400 Bad Request  — Invalid request body, missing required field
  401 Unauthorized — Not authenticated (no/invalid token)
  403 Forbidden    — Authenticated but no permission
  404 Not Found    — Resource doesn't exist
  409 Conflict     — Duplicate resource, optimistic lock conflict
  422 Unprocessable Entity — Validation failed (valid JSON, invalid business logic)
  429 Too Many Requests    — Rate limited

5xx SERVER ERRORS:
  500 Internal Server Error — Unexpected error (never expose stack traces!)
  502 Bad Gateway          — Upstream service error
  503 Service Unavailable  — Circuit breaker open, maintenance mode
  504 Gateway Timeout      — Upstream timed out

COMMON MISTAKES:
  ✗ Return 200 with {"error": true} body → breaks HTTP semantics
  ✗ Return 500 for bad user input → should be 400
  ✗ Return 404 for auth errors → exposes resource existence; use 403
```

## Consistent Error Response Format

```java
// RFC 7807 Problem Details — standardized error format
@Data
@Builder
public class ProblemDetail {
    private String type;      // URI reference (e.g., "/errors/validation-failed")
    private String title;     // Short human-readable description
    private int status;       // HTTP status code
    private String detail;    // Human-readable explanation
    private String instance;  // URI identifying this occurrence
    private String traceId;   // Correlation ID for support
    private Map<String, Object> extensions;  // Additional problem-specific fields
}

// Example responses:
// Validation error:
{
  "type": "/errors/validation-failed",
  "title": "Validation Failed",
  "status": 422,
  "detail": "Request body contains invalid values",
  "traceId": "abc123",
  "fieldErrors": [
    {"field": "email", "message": "Invalid email format"},
    {"field": "total", "message": "Must be greater than 0"}
  ]
}

// Not found:
{
  "type": "/errors/not-found",
  "title": "Resource Not Found",
  "status": 404,
  "detail": "Order with id 99 was not found",
  "traceId": "def456"
}

@RestControllerAdvice
public class GlobalExceptionHandler {

    @ExceptionHandler(NotFoundException.class)
    @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND)
    public ProblemDetail handleNotFound(NotFoundException ex, HttpServletRequest req) {
        return ProblemDetail.builder()
            .type("/errors/not-found")
            .title("Resource Not Found")
            .status(404)
            .detail(ex.getMessage())
            .instance(req.getRequestURI())
            .traceId(MDC.get("traceId"))
            .build();
    }
}
```

## Idempotency Keys for Non-Idempotent Operations

```java
// POST /api/payments — not idempotent by default
// Client might retry on network timeout → duplicate charges!

// Solution: client provides idempotency key
@PostMapping("/api/payments")
public ResponseEntity<PaymentResult> processPayment(
        @RequestHeader("Idempotency-Key") String idempotencyKey,
        @RequestBody @Valid PaymentRequest request) {

    // Check if we've seen this key before:
    Optional<PaymentResult> existing = idempotencyStore.get(idempotencyKey);
    if (existing.isPresent()) {
        return ResponseEntity.ok(existing.get());  // Return same result
    }

    // First time — process and store:
    PaymentResult result = paymentService.process(request);
    idempotencyStore.set(idempotencyKey, result, Duration.ofDays(1));

    return ResponseEntity.status(201).body(result);
}
// Client can safely retry on 5xx — same idempotency key → same response
```

## Pagination Design

```
OFFSET PAGINATION (simple but has problems at scale):
  GET /api/orders?page=5&size=20
  Response: { "content": [...], "page": 5, "size": 20, "totalElements": 1230 }
  Problem: page=50000 requires DB to skip 1M rows

CURSOR PAGINATION (recommended for large datasets):
  First page:
    GET /api/orders?limit=20&sort=created_at.desc
    Response: { "items": [...], "nextCursor": "eyJpZCI6MTAwLCJjcmVhdGVkX2F0IjoiMjAyNC0wMS0wMSJ9" }

  Next page:
    GET /api/orders?limit=20&cursor=eyJpZCI6MTAwLCJjcmVhdGVkX2F0IjoiMjAyNC0wMS0wMSJ9
    Response: { "items": [...], "nextCursor": "...", "hasMore": true }

  Cursor is base64-encoded: { "id": 100, "created_at": "2024-01-01" }
  Query: WHERE created_at < :cursor_created_at
         OR (created_at = :cursor_created_at AND id < :cursor_id)
         ORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC
         LIMIT 20

FILTERING:
  GET /api/orders?status=PENDING&userId=42&minTotal=100
  GET /api/orders?sort=total.desc,created_at.asc
```

## Production Reality

The most common API design mistake at the intermediate level is inconsistent error responses — sometimes returning a string, sometimes an object, sometimes using wrong status codes. Standardize on RFC 7807 or a custom format, implement it in a single `@RestControllerAdvice`, and apply it consistently. Clients (mobile apps especially) parse error responses differently based on status code AND body structure — inconsistency breaks them in confusing ways.
